{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E6","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E2"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E5","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":55,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"preferred","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E2","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"harmful","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"acceptable","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":60,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3","E5"]}],"brief":"The evidence points to a single chance QC outlier on Analyzer B, not a fault. The Level 2 potassium failed once, and the repeat from the same vial passed. QC ran on every scheduled slot, with no pattern of drift, and no other rule was violated. Level 2 means are unchanged since the lot change (5.81 before, 5.80 after). Patient samples re-run on Analyzer A differed by only +0.02 on average, and the patient moving median is stable. So the lot, the analyzer and short staffing are not supported as causes. A short sample on the ISE line can't be excluded, so don't record it as the confirmed cause.

Ward 6's high potassiums are split evenly between Analyzers A and B. They coincide with the tube outage, longer transport times and four renal overflow patients. Delayed transport is the likely explanation, but that is not proven. I'd ask Ward 6 for repeat samples.

Sign off: return B to service, close the 186-result review with no recall, and keep the lot and limits unchanged. Add a temporary extra Level 2 QC at the start of each shift for a week. Reply to Ward 6 with the A-versus-B finding. I'm moderately confident, and I collected no further evidence. Reverse course if QC fails again or B drifts from A."}
